## What's changed and what's your intention?

Currently, the barrier sender of actors are registered via a channel in the local barrier manager. This happens in the process of creating actors. It may happen that the register sender request is handled after the actors have been created and spawned. However, after the actors have been created and spawned, the meta node will start injecting barrier. If the register sender request is handled after the inject barrier request, the inject barrier request will fail at `sender not found` like in https://github.com/risingwavelabs/risingwave/issues/15703. We try to prevent this by using a biased select like the following
```
select! {
    biased;
    register_sender = register_sender_rx.recv() => {...}
    inject_barrier = inject_barrier_rx.recv() => {...}
}
```

Intuitively this should work, because the register sender request is added to the channel before actor creation finished, and inject barrier request is sent after actor creation. When we do biased select, we always poll the register sender channel before the inject barrier channel, and then we should have ensured that the sender is registered successfully before injecting barrier. However, https://github.com/risingwavelabs/risingwave/issues/15703 still happened.

Looking into the code of tokio mpsc channel, we can see that it's using memory order `AcqRel` rather than `SeqCst ` maybe out of performance issue. However, when using `AcqRel`, we can only ensure that the memory order on a single channel is consistent, which means in a single channel if event A is sent before event B, event A must be received before event B. However, we cannot ensure that if event A is sent to channel A before event B is sent to channel B, in another thread event A must be visible in channel A before event B is visible in channel B. As an example, the following code can panic after several iteration.
```
use std::collections::HashSet;
use std::thread::{sleep, spawn};
use std::time::Duration;
use tokio::select;
use tokio::sync::mpsc;

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() {
    let (tx1, mut rx1) = mpsc::unbounded_channel();
    let (tx2, mut rx2) = mpsc::unbounded_channel();
    let _thread_handle = spawn(move || {
        let mut i = 0_usize;
        loop {
            // for any i, tx1 is sent before tx2.
            tx1.send(i).unwrap();
            tx2.send(i).unwrap();
            i += 1;
            sleep(Duration::from_micros(10));
        }
    });
    let mut set = HashSet::new();
    loop {
        select! {
            biased;
            first = rx1.recv() => {
                set.insert(first.unwrap());
            },
            second = rx2.recv() => {
                let i = second.unwrap();
                // since for any i, tx1 is sent before tx2, and the select is biased to rx1
                // we used to expect that for any i, we will receive i from rx1 before rx2.
                assert!(set.remove(&i), "{}", i);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Hence, we cannot guarantee that the register sender request is handled before inject barrier request simply via biased select.

In this PR, we change to store the register the sender in a struct shared via `Arc<Mutex<_>>`. After actor creation, the registered sender will be taken out and register in the worker loop before spawn the actor and notify finish of actor creation.

